Once a ref test is otherwise done, Ladybird’s test-web waits until no
image the document fetched is still loading or decoding, polling on
timers. No other harness checks for loading images. WPT’s own runner
waits two animation frames after reftest-wait is removed, in its
wait_paints() (tools/wptrunner/wptrunner/executors/test-wait.js), and
Gecko’s reftest harness waits for any pending paint, through
isMozAfterPaintPending in its MakeProgress() state machine
(layout/tools/reftest/reftest-content.js).

Neither approach works for test-web: Some ref tests hold rendering back
after removing reftest-wait, so animation frames never come, and a
test’s fonts may never finish loading. So test-web waits on the image
loads themselves — the one thing the capture needs.
